"Use auto property" (IDE0032) ignores how nested types use the field it removes. A nested type can read, write or pass by `ref` the private fields of its containing type, and the fix then rewrites those uses to the property. Depending on the property, that gives code that doesn't compile or code that behaves differently. I didn't find an existing issue for this.

## Repro

A write from a nested type that skips the setter on purpose (C# 14):

```cs
class Class
{
    int i;

    int P
    {
        get => i;
        set
        {
            i = value;
            Changed();
        }
    }

    void Changed() { }

    class Nested
    {
        void Reset(Class c)
        {
            c.i = 0;
        }
    }
}
```

The fix produces `int P { get; set { field = value; Changed(); } }` and rewrites `c.i = 0` to `c.P = 0`. That compiles, but `Reset` now calls `Changed()`. With an `INotifyPropertyChanged` setter, this raises events the code never raised before.

A field passed by `ref` from a nested type:

```cs
class Class
{
    int i;

    int P
    {
        get
        {
            return i;
        }
    }

    class Nested
    {
        void M(Class c)
        {
            System.Threading.Interlocked.Increment(ref c.i);
        }
    }
}
```

The fix produces `int P { get; set; }` and `Interlocked.Increment(ref c.P)`, which fails with CS0206.

If the same uses are in `Class` itself, the analyzer already sees them and doesn't offer the fix.

## Cause

The analyzer runs from a `SymbolKind.NamedType` symbol start action and records field uses with a code block action registered there. Those actions only run on the members of the type itself, not on the members of its nested types, so `fieldReads`, `fieldWrites` and `ineligibleFieldUsageIfOutsideProperty` never contain the nested uses.

## Fix

Nested types now record their uses into the state of each containing type, following what `CSharpUsePrimaryConstructorDiagnosticAnalyzer` does:

- A dictionary created at compilation start maps a type that has nested types to its state. Types without nested types skip the dictionary.
- When a nested type starts, it gets or creates the state of each containing type and registers a code block action that records into it, using the same `RecordIneligibleFieldLocations` and `RecordAllFieldReferences` as before.
- The driver runs a type's symbol end action only after its nested types have been analyzed, so `Process` sees every use. The symbol end action also removes the type from the dictionary.

The per-type collections moved into a small `NamedTypeState` class, and `ShouldAnalyze` became a method so it can run for a containing type. The checks in `Process` are unchanged.

This is the same blind spot as the one fixed for IDE0330 in #86406.

## Tests

New tests:

- `TestNotWhenWrittenFromNestedTypeWithNonTrivialSetter` and `TestNotWhenReadFromNestedTypeWithNonTrivialGetter` in `UseAutoPropertyTests_Field.cs`
- `TestNotWhenPassedByRefFromNestedType` and `TestReadFromNestedType` in `UseAutoPropertyTests.cs`. The second checks that a plain read from a nested type is still offered and rewritten to `c.P`.

The first three fail on `main` and pass with this change. Locally, with analyzers enabled, all 213 tests matching `UseAutoProperty` in `Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp.Features.UnitTests` (net10.0) and all 74 in `Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.VisualBasic.CodeStyle.UnitTests` (net472) pass. `Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CodeStyle` builds, and `dotnet format whitespace --verify-no-changes` passes on the changed files.

One related case is not covered: the `init` and VB checks in `Process` treat a write inside any constructor as a constructor write, so a write from a nested type's constructor still counts as one. Telling the two apart needs the constructor's containing type, which `Process` doesn't have, so I left it out of this change.

