This fixes two bugs in the "Use `System.Threading.Lock`" analyzer (IDE0330). I didn't find an existing issue for either.

## 1. Uses of the field from a nested type are ignored

The analyzer runs per named type, from a symbol start action. The operation actions it registers there see the members of that type but not the members of its nested types, and a nested type can use the outer type's private fields. Those uses were never checked.

Repro:

```cs
using System.Threading;

class C
{
    private object _gate = new object();

    void M()
    {
        lock (_gate) { }
    }

    class Nested
    {
        void N(C c)
        {
            Monitor.Wait(c._gate);
        }
    }
}
```

IDE0330 is reported on `_gate`, and the fix changes the field to `private Lock _gate = new Lock();` while `Monitor.Wait(c._gate)` stays as it is. The only sign of trouble is warning CS9216 on the converted argument. If that warning is missed, `lock (_gate)` now enters the `Lock` instead of the object's monitor, and `Monitor.Wait` throws `SynchronizationLockException` at run time. `Monitor.Pulse`, `Monitor.TryEnter` and passing the field to any other method have the same problem. The same code inside `C` itself is already handled: the analyzer sees the argument use and does not report.

Fix: nested types now check the candidate fields of their containing types, the same way `CSharpUsePrimaryConstructorDiagnosticAnalyzer` handles them. A type that has nested types stores its candidate fields in a dictionary created at compilation start, and each nested type registers a field reference action for the candidates of every containing type. The driver runs a type's symbol end action only after its nested types have been analyzed, so every use is known before the diagnostic is reported. A `lock (c._gate)` in a nested type still counts as a lock, so that case is still offered.

## 2. The option is only checked for the first field in each file

The candidate fields are collected in a loop that reads the option once per syntax tree:

```cs
if (fieldSyntaxTree != currentSyntaxTree)
{
    currentSyntaxTree = fieldSyntaxTree;
    currentOption = context.GetCSharpAnalyzerOptions(currentSyntaxTree).PreferSystemThreadingLock;

    // Ignore this field if it is is in a file that should be skipped.
    if (!currentOption.Value || ShouldSkipAnalysis(currentSyntaxTree, ...))
        continue;
}
```

The `continue` skips only the field that triggered the read. The next field in the same tree doesn't enter the block and is added as a candidate. With `csharp_prefer_system_threading_lock = false`, a class with two private `object` fields used in `lock` statements still gets IDE0330 on the second field, at the severity set on the option.

Fix: the loop now remembers whether the current tree is skipped and applies that to every field in it.

## Changes

`CSharpUseSystemThreadingLockDiagnosticAnalyzer`:

- The code that collects candidate fields moved to `GetPotentialLockFields`, and the field reference check moved to `AnalyzeFieldReference`, so that a type and its nested types can both use them. Apart from the option check, their logic is unchanged.
- `AnalyzeNamedType` registers `AnalyzeFieldReference` for the candidates of each containing type, and removes the type's entry from the dictionary in its symbol end action.

## Tests

New tests in `UseSystemThreadingLockTests`:

- `TestNotWhenOptionDisabled_MultipleFields`
- `TestNotWhenUsedByNestedType`
- `TestNotWhenUsedByDoublyNestedType`
- `TestLockedByNestedType`, still offered
- `TestNestedTypeWithItsOwnLock`, still offered

The first three fail on `main` and pass with this change. Locally, all 53 tests matching `UseSystemThreadingLock` in `Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp.Features.UnitTests` (net10.0) pass with analyzers enabled, `Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp.CodeStyle` builds, and `dotnet format whitespace --verify-no-changes` passes on both files.

